/// \defgroup grp_w1022_contain_body_module_factories Contain and body modules and their factory functions
/// \ingroup grp_objects_modules
/// \brief Eleven contain modules of the horde, garrison and siege families,
///        ActiveBody and seven body modules built on it, the instance
///        factories of all of them except GarrisonContain and ActiveBody,
///        and the INI data factories of seven of their module data classes.
///
/// A **contain module** lets its object hold other objects, such as a
/// building holding a garrison or a horde holding its individual soldiers
/// (\ref sub_objects_modules_pieces). A **body
/// module** keeps its object's health and damage state. Zero Hour:
/// Object::Object asserts, in debug builds only, that an object does not
/// get a second body module.
/// Object INI names either kind on a module line (`Behavior = HordeContain
/// ModuleTag_...`, `Body = ActiveBody ModuleTag_...`). This group documents
/// the classes at the level of their role and INI contract, and the code
/// that creates the modules and their data. The containers' and bodies'
/// own logic (OpenContain, TransportContain and ActiveBody internals) is
/// separate reverse-engineering work and is not described here. BFME 2
/// registers other contain and body modules, some of them derived from the
/// classes here; only the classes listed below are covered.
///
/// \par The factory flow
/// ModuleFactory::init registers each module name with an instance
/// factory, a data factory, a module type and an interface mask; the
/// registrations are in `reverse/module_factory_registrations.csv`. The
/// data factory runs while INI loads (ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI),
/// the instance factory when an object is built (ModuleFactory::newModule).
/// \ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories describes this flow step by
/// step and \ref grp_w1021_upgrade_module_factories describes the shared
/// code shape of both factories; both apply here unchanged. Neither factory
/// is called directly: nothing reaches them except through the registered
/// pointers.
///
/// \par Registrations
/// - Every name in this group is spelled exactly like its class and is
///   registered as a logic (behavior) module.
/// - All contain modules here share one interface mask, the same value as
///   every other registration whose name ends in "Contain" (OpenContain,
///   TransportContain and CaveContain among them).
///   Read with Zero Hour's bit values (`Common/Module.h`), it is Zero Hour's
///   OpenContain set (update, die, damage, collide, contain) plus the create
///   bit; the BFME 2 meaning of the bits is not confirmed here.
/// - All body modules here share one mask, equal to Zero Hour's body
///   interface bit alone. Zero Hour: a `Body =` line must name a module with
///   that bit and a `Behavior =` line must not (ThingTemplate's module
///   parser checks this). BFME 2's thing template table passes the same
///   marker value on its `Body` key as Zero Hour; whether the BFME 2 parser
///   enforces the same rule is not checked here.
/// - A data factory can serve several names. ActiveBody, HighlanderBody,
///   ImmortalBody and OathbreakerBody all register
///   ActiveBodyModuleData::friend_newModuleData, so they read the same
///   fields. HorseHordeContain registers its own data factory (not
///   documented here), which builds a data class derived from
///   HordeContainModuleData and parses it with HordeContainModuleData's
///   field table.
///
/// \par Class families (classes in this group)
/// The base classes below come from the classes' constructors, and the INI
/// inheritance from the data classes' \c buildFieldParse functions, each
/// of which adds its parent's fields before its own. In every case below
/// the two chains agree. The bullets place only the classes this group
/// documents (and the bases they need); they are not complete lists of
/// each base's subclasses.
/// - OpenContain, then TransportContain, then HordeContain (with
///   HorseHordeContain and AODHordeContain derived from it),
///   SiegeEngineContain and HordeTransportContain (with
///   HordeSiegeEngineContain derived from it).
/// - OpenContain, then GarrisonContain, then HordeGarrisonContain, with
///   SlaughterHordeContain (and CitadelSlaughterHordeContain derived from
///   it) and ProductionQueueHordeContain derived from it. Despite the
///   names, this branch does not derive from HordeContain.
/// - ActiveBody, with OathbreakerBody, RespawnBody, DetachableRiderBody,
///   SymbioticStructuresBody and PorcupineFormationBodyModule derived from
///   it; DelayedDeathBody and FreeLifeBody derive from RespawnBody.
///
/// Classes outside this group also derive from these bases. Their
/// constructors show, among others, TunnelContain deriving from
/// HordeGarrisonContain, RiderChangeContain from SiegeEngineContain, and
/// HighlanderBody, ImmortalBody and StructureBody from ActiveBody; none of
/// them is documented here.
///
/// \par Implementation notes common to the factories
/// - Each instance factory documented here allocates the module, runs the
///   class's constructor with the object and the module data inside an
///   exception frame that frees the block if the constructor throws, and
///   returns the module as the Module base the factory stores; when the
///   allocation returns null, the constructor is skipped and null is
///   returned.
/// - Each data factory documented here allocates the data, runs its
///   constructor (defaults) under the same kind of exception frame, and,
///   when the INI reader is not null, parses the module's block into it
///   with INI::initFromINIMultiProc and the class's static
///   \c buildFieldParse. Parsing happens after the frame, so an INI error
///   thrown while parsing does not free the new data. With a null reader
///   the data keeps its constructor defaults (Zero Hour uses that to give
///   a template default modules without INI).
/// - A factory's identity comes from its ModuleFactory::init
///   registration, not from the name of the file that holds it.
///
/// \par Origins
/// GarrisonContain and ActiveBody exist in Zero Hour; none of the other
/// classes here does.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Data-driven: which objects carry these modules, their tags, and every
///   field listed on the class pages, including the fields inherited from
///   the parent classes' tables. A horde's rank and formation settings, a
///   garrison's \c InitialRoster and the siege containers' crew fields all
///   come from the INI block.
/// - Hard-coded: the module names, the class hierarchy (which fields a
///   name accepts) and what each class does with its fields. A new kind of
///   container or body needs a new class and a new ModuleFactory::init
///   registration (\ref page_modding_modules).
/// - Fields marked as text below are stored as text (INI::parseAsciiString
///   or INI::parseAsciiStringVector). Judging by their names they hold
///   names of other things, among them upgrades, attribute modifiers,
///   formations and objects. This page does not establish when or how
///   those names are looked up, or whether a misspelled name is reported.
/// - Multiplayer: these modules run in the game logic, so every machine
///   must load identical data (\ref page_modding_rules).
/// - Saves: each module saves its state under its module tag
///   (\ref sub_objects_modules_lifecycle); \ref grp_w3_module_xfer
///   documents the transfer chain of the slaughter containers.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// Fields marked as durations below are parsed by
/// INI::parseDurationUnsignedInt, which converts milliseconds into whole
/// logic frames once, when the INI is read
/// (\ref grp_w1020_special_power_module_factories has the details);
/// GarrisonContain's \c TimeForFullHeal uses INI::parseDurationReal
/// instead. A change to the logic rate (\ref page_frame_loop) changes what
/// those stored frame counts mean.
///
/// \see ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI, ModuleFactory::newModule,
///      INI::initFromINIMultiProc, \ref sub_objects_modules,
///      \ref sub_rts_model_flow


/// \class HordeContain
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_contain_body_module_factories
/// \brief Contain module registered as "HordeContain": the container that
///        makes one object a horde of soldiers.
///
/// A horde is a single object whose HordeContain (or a subclass) holds its
/// individual members as contained objects (\ref sub_objects_modules_pieces).
/// It derives from TransportContain, so its INI block accepts every
/// OpenContain and TransportContain field as well as its own.
/// In this group, HorseHordeContain and AODHordeContain derive from it.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields (HordeContainModuleData), read from the retail table, in its
/// order: \c ThisFormationIsTheMainFormation, \c RankInfo,
/// \c RanksThatStopAdvance, \c RanksToReleaseWhenAttacking,
/// \c RanksToJustFreeWhenAttacking, \c ComboHorde, \c AlternateFormation
/// (text), \c RandomOffset (2D coordinate), \c LeaderPosition (3D
/// coordinate), \c LeadersAllowed (text list), \c BannerCarrierPosition,
/// \c BannerCarriersAllowed (text list), \c LeaderRank (integer),
/// \c BackUpMinDelayTime and \c BackUpMaxDelayTime (durations),
/// \c BackUpMinDistance and \c BackUpMaxDistance (reals),
/// \c BackupPercentage (percent), \c CowerRadius (real),
/// \c AttributeModifiers (text list), \c IsPorcupineFormation,
/// \c ForcedLocomotorSet (one name from a fixed list), \c MachineAllowed,
/// \c MachineType (text), \c UseSlowHordeMovement, \c SplitHorde,
/// \c MeleeAttackLeashDistance
/// (real), \c EvaEventLastMemberDeath (an Eva event), \c RankSplit,
/// \c SplitHordeNumber (integer), \c NotComboFormation,
/// \c UseMarchingAnims, \c FrontAngle (real), \c FlankedDelay and
/// \c FlankedDuration (durations), \c MeleeBehavior (dedicated parser,
/// below), \c MinimumHordeSize (unsigned integer), \c VisionRearOverride
/// and \c VisionSideOverride (percents). Fields
/// without a type here are booleans or use dedicated parsers;
/// \ref grp_w3_ini_field_callbacks documents the banner-carrier and
/// split-result parsers.
///
/// \c MeleeBehavior is a name followed by a nested block, not a single
/// value. Its parser, HordeContainModuleData::parseMeleeBehavior, reads
/// one name from the fixed set \c Swarm, \c WaitForLeader,
/// \c HoldGround and \c Amoeba (INI::parseIndexList), creates the
/// matching behaviour object, has that object build its own field table,
/// and reads the following lines into it with INI::initFromINIMulti. In
/// the Zero Hour shape that function reads field lines until the block's
/// end token (\c END, compared without regard to case). The name set is
/// hard-coded (\ref sub_objects_modules_modding); the behaviour classes
/// and their own fields are not described here.
///
/// \see HordeContain::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *HordeContain::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "HordeContain": creates the
///        container for one horde object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration when an
/// object whose template lists the module is built. The group page
/// describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's HordeContainModuleData for this module
///                    line, shared with every other object of the template.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see HordeContain


/// \class HorseHordeContain
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_contain_body_module_factories
/// \brief Contain module registered as "HorseHordeContain": a HordeContain
///        subclass that reads exactly the HordeContain fields.
///
/// Its data class derives from HordeContainModuleData and keeps its field
/// table, so it adds no INI fields of its own. The name suggests horde
/// containers for mounted units; the behaviour that differs from
/// HordeContain is not described here.
///
/// \see HorseHordeContain::friend_newModuleInstance, HordeContain

/// \fn Module *HorseHordeContain::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "HorseHordeContain": creates the
///        container for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see HorseHordeContain


/// \class AODHordeContain
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_contain_body_module_factories
/// \brief Contain module registered as "AODHordeContain": a HordeContain
///        subclass with motion parameters for its members.
///
/// It accepts every HordeContain field plus its own. Its own fields are
/// all reals except one duration; their names describe frequencies,
/// amplitudes and randomness (each also in a Z variant), limits for large
/// units, and scattering. The code that applies them is
/// not described here. The name and the field \c OathFulfilledZFactor
/// suggest the Army of the Dead, which this page does not confirm.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields (AODHordeContainModuleData), read from the retail table:
/// \c FrequencyScale, \c FrequencyRandomness, \c AmplitudeScale,
/// \c AmplitudeRandomness, \c StillAmplitude, \c FrequencyScaleZ,
/// \c FrequencyRandomnessZ, \c AmplitudeScaleZ, \c AmplitudeRandomnessZ,
/// \c StillAmplitudeZ, \c OathFulfilledZFactor, \c LargeUnitHeightFactor,
/// \c LargeUnitMinHeight, \c LargeUnitMaxHeight, \c LargeUnitTimeout
/// (duration), \c LargeUnitTailOff, \c ScatterSpeedFactor and
/// \c ScatterRandomness.
///
/// \note The retail table lists \c FrequencyRandomness and
///       \c FrequencyRandomnessZ twice each, every copy with the same
///       parser and destination, so the duplicates are harmless.
///
/// \see AODHordeContain::friend_newModuleInstance, HordeContain

/// \fn Module *AODHordeContain::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "AODHordeContain": creates the
///        container for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's AODHordeContainModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see AODHordeContain


/// \class SiegeEngineContain
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_contain_body_module_factories
/// \brief Contain module registered as "SiegeEngineContain": a
///        TransportContain with crew fields.
///
/// It accepts every OpenContain and TransportContain field plus its own
/// crew fields. Their names describe which objects may crew the engine,
/// how many, the starting crew, a speed percentage per crew member and
/// whether the crew may fire; the code that uses them is not described
/// here. RiderChangeContain, which is not documented here, derives from
/// it; HordeSiegeEngineContain does not (see its page).
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields (SiegeEngineContainModuleData), read from the retail table:
/// \c CrewFilter (object filter), \c CrewMax (integer), \c InitialCrew
/// (dedicated parser), \c SpeedPercentPerCrew (percent),
/// \c CrewAllowedToFire (boolean), \c ObjectStatusOfCrew (object status
/// bits) and \c TransferSelection (boolean).
///
/// \see SiegeEngineContain::friend_newModuleInstance, HordeSiegeEngineContain

/// \fn Module *SiegeEngineContain::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "SiegeEngineContain": creates the
///        container for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's SiegeEngineContainModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see SiegeEngineContain


/// \class HordeTransportContain
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_contain_body_module_factories
/// \brief Contain module registered as "HordeTransportContain": a
///        TransportContain subclass with no INI fields of its own.
///
/// Its data class's \c buildFieldParse only forwards to TransportContain's,
/// so its INI block accepts exactly the OpenContain and TransportContain
/// fields. Its constructor adds state of its own on top of TransportContain;
/// what the class does differently is not described here.
/// In this group, HordeSiegeEngineContain derives from it.
///
/// \see HordeTransportContain::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *HordeTransportContain::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "HordeTransportContain": creates
///        the container for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's module data for this module line.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see HordeTransportContain


/// \class HordeSiegeEngineContain
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_contain_body_module_factories
/// \brief Contain module registered as "HordeSiegeEngineContain": a
///        container with crew fields, built on HordeTransportContain rather
///        than on SiegeEngineContain.
///
/// It accepts the OpenContain and TransportContain fields (through
/// HordeTransportContain) plus crew fields of its own. Those are the same
/// keys as SiegeEngineContain's, read with the same parsers, except that
/// \c TransferSelection is not among them. Neither class derives from the
/// other; both reach TransportContain by different paths.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields (HordeSiegeEngineContainModuleData), read from the retail
/// table: \c CrewFilter, \c CrewMax, \c InitialCrew,
/// \c SpeedPercentPerCrew, \c CrewAllowedToFire and \c ObjectStatusOfCrew
/// (types as on SiegeEngineContain).
///
/// \see HordeSiegeEngineContain::friend_newModuleInstance, SiegeEngineContain

/// \fn Module *HordeSiegeEngineContain::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "HordeSiegeEngineContain":
///        creates the container for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's HordeSiegeEngineContainModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see HordeSiegeEngineContain


/// \class GarrisonContain
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_contain_body_module_factories
/// \brief Contain module registered as "GarrisonContain": an OpenContain
///        for objects, usually structures, that units can garrison.
///
/// Zero Hour: the class places its occupants at fire points on the
/// structure and can heal them. In BFME 2 its INI block accepts every
/// OpenContain field plus the five below. In this group,
/// HordeGarrisonContain derives from it. Its instance factory is not
/// documented here.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields (GarrisonContainModuleData), read from the retail table:
/// \c MobileGarrison and \c HealObjects (booleans), \c TimeForFullHeal
/// (duration read as a real), \c InitialRoster (an object name and a
/// count) and \c ImmuneToClearBuildingAttacks (boolean). Zero Hour's
/// table has the same five fields plus \c IsEnclosingContainer, which is
/// not in BFME 2's table.
///
/// \see GarrisonContainModuleData::friend_newModuleData, HordeGarrisonContain

/// \class GarrisonContainModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_contain_body_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for GarrisonContain: the OpenContain fields plus the
///        garrison fields.
///
/// Built once per module line by GarrisonContainModuleData::friend_newModuleData
/// and shared by every object of the template. In this group,
/// HordeGarrisonContainModuleData derives from it.

/// \fn ModuleData *GarrisonContainModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "GarrisonContain": creates the module
///        data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration
/// for each module line naming GarrisonContain. The group page describes
/// the shared pattern.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see GarrisonContain


/// \class HordeGarrisonContain
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_contain_body_module_factories
/// \brief Contain module registered as "HordeGarrisonContain": a
///        GarrisonContain with entry and exit positions.
///
/// It derives from GarrisonContain (not from HordeContain) and accepts
/// every OpenContain and GarrisonContain field plus four of its own.
/// HordeGarrisonContain::xfer transfers its own version, then
/// GarrisonContain's block, then one value of its own only when the version
/// (on load, the one read from the stream) is 2 or later, so a version-1
/// record loads without it (\ref grp_w3_module_xfer describes the chain).
/// In this group, SlaughterHordeContain and ProductionQueueHordeContain
/// derive from it; TunnelContain, which is not documented here, also
/// does.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields (HordeGarrisonContainModuleData), read from the retail table:
/// \c ExitDelay (duration) and \c EntryOffset, \c EntryPosition and
/// \c ExitOffset (3D coordinates). TransportContain's table also has an
/// \c ExitDelay key; this class does not derive from TransportContain, so
/// the two do not collide.
///
/// \see HordeGarrisonContain::friend_newModuleInstance,
///      HordeGarrisonContainModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \class HordeGarrisonContainModuleData
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_contain_body_module_factories
/// \brief Module data for HordeGarrisonContain: the garrison fields plus the
///        entry and exit positions.
///
/// Built once per module line by
/// HordeGarrisonContainModuleData::friend_newModuleData and shared by every
/// object of the template. The data classes of SlaughterHordeContain and
/// ProductionQueueHordeContain chain their field tables onto this one, and
/// so does TunnelContain's (not documented here).

/// \fn Module *HordeGarrisonContain::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "HordeGarrisonContain": creates
///        the container for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
/// page describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param thing       The object being built; the module keeps it as its
///                    owner.
/// \param moduleData  The template's HordeGarrisonContainModuleData.
/// \return The new module as the Module base the factory stores, or null
///         when the allocation fails.
/// \see HordeGarrisonContainModuleData::friend_newModuleData

/// \fn ModuleData *HordeGarrisonContainModuleData::friend_newModuleData(INI *ini)
/// \brief Data factory registered for "HordeGarrisonContain": creates the
///        module data and parses the module's INI block into it.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModuleDataFromINI calls it through the registration.
/// The group page describes the shared pattern.
///
/// \param ini  The reader positioned on the module's block, or null to
///             keep the constructor defaults.
/// \return The new module data as the ModuleData base the factory stores.
/// \see HordeGarrisonContain::friend_newModuleInstance


/// \class SlaughterHordeContain
/// \ingroup grp_w1022_contain_body_module_factories
/// \brief Contain module registered as "SlaughterHordeContain": a
///        HordeGarrisonContain with cash-back and forced-entry fields.
///
/// It accepts every field of its HordeGarrisonContain chain plus three of
/// its own. The field names suggest that it pays back a percentage
/// (\c CashBackPercent) for what enters it and that objects passing a
/// filter, with a required status, can always enter; the code that does so
/// is not described here. Its save block is documented under
/// SlaughterHordeContain::xfer (\ref grp_w3_module_xfer). In this group,
/// CitadelSlaughterHordeContain derives from it.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Own fields (SlaughterHordeContainModuleData), read from the retail
/// table: \c CashBackPercent (percent), \c CanAlwaysEnter (object filter)
/// and \c StatusRequiredForCanAlwaysEnter (object status bits).
///
/// \see SlaughterHordeContain::friend_newModuleInstance

/// \fn Module *SlaughterHordeContain::friend_newModuleInstance(Thing *thing, const ModuleData *moduleData)
/// \brief Instance factory registered for "SlaughterHordeContain": creates
///        the container for one object.
///
/// ModuleFactory::newModule calls it through the registration. The group
